# BOHEMIA SIDE QUEST — PLAYABLE .bq (canon machine layer)
# S24 — FIFTY-FIVE GALLONS A FOOT
# Shape: the accusation quest, where the crime is arithmetic and the arithmetic
# is right. Faction wires: BLUES, TRADES, VOLUNTEERS. Grounded in a real Las
# Vegas economic instrument that ran for twenty-five years and taught an entire
# city to price grass in water:
#   The Southern Nevada Water Authority's Water Smart Landscapes rebate paid
#   people to tear out their lawns -- $5 a square foot from SNWA, up to $7 with
#   the water district's share, on the first 10,000 feet. Since 1999 the valley
#   converted more than 185 MILLION square feet and saved over 119 billion
#   gallons. The number in every homeowner's head, printed on every flyer:
#   about 55 GALLONS SAVED PER SQUARE FOOT PER YEAR.
#   (snwa.com/rebates/wsl)
# THE ECONOMY DIED AND THE INSTRUMENT DID NOT. Everybody on this block still
# converts grass to gallons in their head, instantly, the way you read a price
# tag -- because for a generation they were paid to. So a lawn that is still
# green is not pretty here. It is a number, and everyone has already done it.
# Systems AS the mechanic: the whole quest is one measurement anybody can take
# by pacing it out, and the answer is correct. The green square really is
# drinking that much. What the number cannot tell you is where it comes from.
# The lesson underneath, never spoken: being right about the arithmetic is not
# the same as being right. No proper names. CLOUT rides the deed: going to the
# door yourself is #quiet, calling the block's meeting is #notable, cutting the
# supply line is #reckless.

# ---- QUESTBOOK STUDY CITATIONS (QUEST STUDY LAW, 7/26/26) -------------------
# The corpus this quest is actually built from. Every id below resolves in
# records/BOHEMIA_QUESTBOOK_LAW_INDEX.json (3,672 citable findings mined out of
# the 152 studied quests in questbook/), and gates/quest_study_gate.js verifies
# the id AND the quoted law title verbatim. A citation is a claim the machine
# can check, never a name-drop.
# @STUDY Q026.W5  ACCUSE-WHO-YOU-BELIEVE *OR* WHO-DESERVES-IT
#   applied: the block does not want the truth about the lawn, it wants a verdict, and it will take yours. Both available answers are answers to different questions.
# @STUDY Q026.W6  YOU LIVE WITH BEING (MAYBE) WRONG
#   applied: no ending in this quest confirms you. The greywater rig is never shown to the player as proven, only claimed, and the game never settles it.
# @STUDY Q026.P5  W5 (accuse-who-you-believe OR who-deserves-it
#   applied: the flaws-master's version of the same law is the one that shapes the market ending -- naming somebody in front of people is a different act from believing it.
# @STUDY Q019.W3  THE HOLISTIC SCENE-READ
#   applied: the evidence is the yard itself. Pace the edges, look at where the wet line runs, notice the drain side of the house. Nobody hands you a clue.
# @STUDY Q018.W3  THE RUMOR WEB (curiosity as the quest log)
#   applied: there is no objective marker on the door. The quest exists because three separate people mention the green square without being asked.
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

@QUEST   bq_fifty_five   Fifty-Five Gallons A Foot
@ACT     1
@FACTION Blues
@ONCE    true

@ROLE neighbour REQ  faction=Blues       counts_the_gallons=true
@ROLE owner     REQ  faction=Volunteers  keeps_the_green_square=true
@ROLE plumber   REQ  faction=Trades      knows_greywater=true

@STAGE 10
  @LOG Third person this week has mentioned the green square on the corner without being asked about it. Nobody on this block waters anything.
  @DO show_objective 10
@STAGE 20
  @LOG Paced it. Call it forty by fifty. Two thousand square feet of living grass, and every foot of it's fifty-five gallons a year. The block has already done that sum.
  @DO show_objective 20
  @DO complete_objective 10
@STAGE 30 COMPLETE #quiet
  @LOG Knocked on the door instead of talking about it. There's a greywater rig under the side of that house feeding the whole yard. Told the street it was handled and left it at that.
  @DO bond owner +25
  @DO learn the_grey_rig
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 31 COMPLETE #notable
  @LOG Called the meeting and put the number up: a hundred and ten thousand gallons a year, on a corner lot, in this. Said whose lot it was. Everyone did the arithmetic with me.
  @DO faction Blues +10
  @DO bond owner -20
  @DO set_flag named_at_the_meeting
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 32 COMPLETE #reckless
  @LOG Cut the line into that yard in daylight with people watching. The grass will be straw in nine days. Whatever was feeding it isn't feeding anything now, including the parts of that house I didn't look at.
  @DO faction Blues +6
  @DO bond owner -40
  @DO set_flag cut_the_green_square
  @DO advance_territory
  @DO complete_objective 20
@STAGE 33 FAIL #quiet
  @LOG Decided it wasn't mine to look into. It's still green. Three people asked me about it again this week and I said I didn't know, which by then was a choice rather than a fact.
  @DO faction Blues -10

@OBJ 10  "Work out what the green square actually costs"  target=neighbour
@OBJ 20  "Settle the green square"                        target=owner

# =====================================================================
# THE RUMOUR. She is not asking you to do anything. She is doing the
# arithmetic out loud, which is how this block asks.
# =====================================================================
@TALK green  speaker=neighbour  entry=stage>=10,stage<20
  @SAY You've seen it. Everybody has seen it. Corner lot, past the dead one with the boat. #wary
  @SAY Forty by fifty, near enough. Two thousand feet. And you know the number, everybody our age knows the number, they printed it on everything for twenty years. Fifty-five gallons a foot a year.
  @SAY That's a hundred and ten thousand gallons. On grass. While I'm rationing what I wash in. I'm not telling you to do anything. I'm telling you the number.
  @OPT "Let me go and look at it properly." [gate: none]  -> yard
  @OPT "Where is it coming from?"           [gate: none]  -> where
  @OPT "The number could be right and the story still be wrong." [gate: none]  -> careful
  @OPT (walk toward the corner)             [gate: none]  SILENCE -> yard
  @NOVERB "Ask if anybody has knocked"
@END

@TALK where  speaker=neighbour
  @SAY That's the part nobody can answer and that's why it's got the teeth it has. #wary
  @SAY There's no tanker. Nobody sees anybody hauling. The mains on that side run at nothing like the pressure you'd need. So either it's a miracle or somebody is taking it from somewhere that has not been noticed yet.
  @OPT "Or from somewhere that isn't the mains." [gate: none]  -> yard
  @OPT "I'll go and look."                      [gate: none]  -> yard
@END

@TALK careful  speaker=neighbour
  @SAY Maybe. #wary
  @SAY But I've been doing that sum every time I walk past for four months and I have not once come up with a version where I'm wrong about the gallons. Go on. Tell me I'm wrong about the gallons.
  @OPT "You aren't. Let me look anyway." [gate: none]  -> yard
  @OPT "Not my business."                 [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 33
@END

# =====================================================================
# THE SCENE-READ. No clue is handed over. The yard is the evidence and
# the evidence is on the side of the house nobody walks down.
# =====================================================================
@TALK yard  speaker=neighbour
  @SAY Green all the way to the fence on three sides. And then the strip along the side of the house, where the drain would be, is the greenest part of the whole thing. #wary
  @OPT "That's the wet line. It's coming out of the house, not into it." [gate: none]  -> rig  @DO learn the_grey_rig
  @OPT "Knock on the door."                                                 [gate: none]  -> door
  @OPT "Call the meeting. Put the number up."                               [gate: none]  -> meeting
  @OPT "Find the line and cut it."                                          [gate: none]  -> cut
  @NOVERB "Say the word greywater"
@END

@TALK rig  speaker=plumber  entry=stage>=20
  @SAY Side of the house, under the vent. That's a greywater rig and a decent one. Wash water, bath water, out the wall, through a filter bed, into the yard. #wary
  @SAY It isn't coming off the mains at all. Whoever built that's using the same water twice and the yard is the second use. Which is the single smartest thing anybody on this street is doing.
  @SAY It also looks exactly like theft from forty feet away, and forty feet is as close as anybody has gone.
  @OPT "Then I go and knock."                [gate: none]  -> door
  @OPT "The block still deserves the number." [gate: none]  -> meeting
@END

# =====================================================================
# THE DOOR. She is not innocent-and-grateful. She has been waiting four
# months for somebody to knock instead of talk, and she is angry about it.
# =====================================================================
@TALK door  speaker=owner
  @SAY Four months. Four months of people slowing down at my fence and speeding up when I come out. You're the first one to use the gate. #wary
  @SAY It's the wash water. All of it. Every drop that yard drinks has already been through this house once. I built it out of a bathtub and forty feet of pipe and I'd show anybody who asked.
  @SAY Nobody asked. They did the sum instead. And the sum is right, that's the thing that's been killing me. It IS a hundred and ten thousand gallons. It's just not YOURS.
  @OPT "Then it's handled. I'll tell them."     [gate: none]  -> handled
  @OPT "Show me the rig."                          [gate: none]  -> handled
  @OPT "Show the block, not me."                   [gate: none]  -> meeting
  @OPT (stand there and say nothing)               [gate: none]  SILENCE -> handled
@END

@TALK handled  speaker=owner
  @SAY You can tell them whatever you like. They'll believe it or they won't. #wary
  @SAY If you want to do me an actual kindness, tell them how it works rather than that it's fine. Half of them have a bathtub and forty feet of pipe.
  @OPT "I'll tell them how it works." [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 30
  @OPT (go and say it was handled)      [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 30
@END

# THE MEETING. Naming somebody in front of people is a different act
# from believing a thing, and the quest holds that difference open.
@TALK meeting  speaker=neighbour
  @SAY You want it in front of everyone. All right. That's a bell you can't un-ring on a corner lot. #wary
  @SAY Say the number and say the address and they'll do the rest. And whatever the answer turns out to be, that house is the house with the green square from now on.
  @OPT "Put the number up. Say whose lot."  [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 31
  @OPT "No. I'm going to knock first."     [gate: none]  -> door
@END

@TALK cut  speaker=neighbour
  @SAY In daylight, with people watching. #dread
  @SAY Understand you won't know what else that line was doing. Water in a house isn't one thing. But it'll be brown in nine days and everybody will have watched you do it.
  @OPT "Cut it."                            [gate: none]  -> END  @DO set_stage 32
  @OPT (take the cutters and go)            [gate: none]  SILENCE -> END  @DO set_stage 32
  @OPT "No. The gate first."                [gate: none]  -> door
@END
